@@ -26597,3 +26597,15 @@ msgstr ""
 msgctxt "#40925"
 msgid "For a display that takes Dolby Vision. An HDR10 video, which has no dynamic metadata of its own, is sent as Dolby Vision with metadata that Hades measures from the picture as it plays: the peak, the average and the darkest level of each scene, and the black bars. These metadata are not the studio's: the studio made no Dolby Vision metadata for the video, and nothing of a colourist's grade (trims) is added. The picture is the same HDR10 picture. Scene cuts are found from the picture: a few are missed, a few are found where there is none, and some are found a picture late. Dolby Vision and HDR10+ videos are not affected. Applies from the next video."
 msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvashdr10plus"
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#40926"
+msgid "Send it as HDR10+ where the display takes it"
+msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvashdr10plus"
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#40927"
+msgid "For a display that takes HDR10+ and not Dolby Vision, as many Samsung TVs. A Dolby Vision video rebuilt as HDR10 is sent as HDR10+: with each scene, dynamic metadata that Hades measures from the rebuilt picture (the average and the distribution of its brightness), so that the display adapts each scene as it adapts an HDR10+ film. The scenes are those of the Dolby Vision metadata. These metadata are not the studio's, and no tone curve is sent: the display makes its own. Profile 8.1 is then rebuilt too; profile 7 as the setting above says. The first picture of a scene goes out with the metadata of the scene before (the picture is measured after it is drawn), and the display may show a scene otherwise than as plain HDR10. Not while the picture is mapped to the display. Applies from the next video."
+msgstr ""
